# No hardcoded "current state" literals in checks (all agents)

## Why this exists

The same bug shape hit this repo twice in one session (24 Jul 2026):

1. **`backend/audit_contracts.json` / `test_audit_service.py`'s enum-count assertions** —
   `test_audit_action_type_enum_has_60_migration_003_values` hardcoded `assert len(...) == 158`.
   The moment a later change (C333) added 7 new `AuditActionType` values, this assertion went
   stale and failed — not because anything was wrong, but because nobody updates a hardcoded count
   when they add an enum value in a completely different file.
2. **`backend/app/core/schema_contract.py`'s `EXPECTED_REVISION = "a146"`** — hardcoded at the time
   C331 shipped, when `a146` was genuinely the Alembic head. Migrations `a147`-`a150` landed in
   later, unrelated sessions with no reason to know this file needed updating. The literal going
   stale **blocked a real production deploy** with a false failure (see
   [issue-187](../../backend/prod_issues/issue-187-schema-contract-expected-revision-hardcoded-stale-literal.md)).

Both are instances of the same anti-pattern: a check that means "does X match the current state of
Y" was implemented by writing down today's value of Y as a literal, instead of computing it from Y
itself. The check then silently diverges from reality every time Y changes somewhere else, and the
person changing Y usually has no reason to know this check exists.

## Rule

When writing a test, verifier, or deploy-time gate that means "the current head/count/set of X":

1. **Prefer deriving the value from the actual source of truth**, not hardcoding it. Concretely:
   - "The current Alembic head" → parse `alembic/versions/*.py`'s revision chain (see
     `docs/agent_rules/alembic-package-name-shadowing.md` for how to do this without an import
     collision), or shell out to the real `alembic heads` command — not a literal revision ID.
   - "The current count of enum values" → `len(SomeEnum)` compared against a computed baseline
     (e.g. a previous count plus an itemised list of the specific additions since, so the
     diff is reviewable), not a bare hardcoded integer with no way to tell why it's that number.
   - "The current set of registered X" → enumerate X programmatically (AST-parse route decorators,
     walk a directory, introspect a registry) and diff against what's declared, rather than listing
     every X by hand somewhere that has to be kept in sync manually.
2. **If deriving the value truly isn't practical**, pair the hardcoded literal with a test that
   cross-checks it against an independent computation of the real value (not the same logic under
   test — an actual independent source, like running the real CLI as a subprocess). This is a
   fallback, not the first choice — `test_expected_revision_matches_real_alembic_head_not_a_stale_literal`
   in `backend/tests/test_c331_schema_contract.py` is the reference pattern if this is genuinely
   the only option.
3. **Before adding a new enum value, migration, route, or registered entity anywhere in this repo,
   check whether an existing hardcoded count/list elsewhere needs updating.** `grep` for the
   plural/count-shaped assertion pattern (`len(...) ==`, `EXPECTED_*`, a bare integer next to
   "count"/"total"/"head") in the area you're touching before assuming your change is isolated.
4. **A failing test that says "expected N, got N+k" where k matches exactly what you just added
   elsewhere is a strong signal you've hit this pattern** — the fix is almost always to update the
   hardcoded expectation to match the new, legitimate reality (with a comment explaining the
   addition), not to silently work around the test.

